"Biden-Harris Administration's National Security Strategy.pdf (https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Biden-Harris-Administrations-National-Security-Strategy-10.2022.pdf)\nciteturn71view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 48; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Biden-Harris-Administrations-National-Security-Strategy-10.2022.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1800\nL0@P0: NATIONAL\nL1@P0: SECURITY\nL2@P0: STRATEGY\nL3@P0-1: OCTOBER 2022October 12, 2022\nL4@P1: From the earliest days of my Presidency, I have argued that our world is at an inflection point.\nL5@P1: How we respond to the tremendous challenges and the unprecedented opportunities we face\nL6@P1: today will determine the direction of our world and impact the security and prosperity of the\nL7@P1: American people for generations to come. The 2022 National Security Strategy outlines how my\nL8@P1: Administration will seize this decisive decade to advance America’s vital interests, position the\nL9@P1: United States to outmaneuver our geopolitical competitors, tackle shared challenges, and set our\nL10@P1: world firmly on a path toward a brighter and more hopeful tomorrow.\nL11@P1: Around the world, the need for American leadership is as great as it has ever been. We are in the\nL12@P1: midst of a strategic competition to shape the future of the international order. Meanwhile, shared\nL13@P1: challenges that impact people everywhere demand increased global cooperation and nations\nL14@P1: stepping up to their responsibilities at a moment when this has become more difficult. In\nL15@P1: response, the United States will lead with our values, and we will work in lockstep with our\nL16@P1: allies and partners and with all those who share our interests. We will not leave our future\nL17@P1: vulnerable to the whims of those who do not share our vision for a world that is free, open,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30702330/)\nciteturn71view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn58search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\nThis page has no readable lines.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnited States (EN) (https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2024/12/survey-of-adults-skills-2023-country-notes_df7b4a60/united-states_efb9f176/427d6aac-en.pdf)\nciteturn71view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.5 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn58search13\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 214\nL15: In literacy, 28% of adults (OECD average: 26%) scored at Level 1 or below, meaning they have low literacy proficiency. At Level 1, they can understand short texts and organised lists when information is clearly indicated, find specific information and identify relevant links. Those below Level 1 can at most understand\nL16: \nL17: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL23: When considering all three domains jointly, 23% of adults in the United States (OECD average: 18%) scored at the two lowest levels of these proficiency scales (Table A.2.3).\nL24: \nL25: Figure 2. Proficiency in literacy, numeracy, and adaptive problem solving among adults\nL26: \nL27: The United States and OECD average\nL28: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL29: Horizontal stacked bar chart comparing the United States and OECD average across three domains: Literacy, Numeracy, and Adaptive Problem Solving. Legend categories are Below Level 1 (dark gray), Level 1 (light gray), Level 2 (light blue), Level 3 (teal), and Level 4 and above (dark blue). Percent labels shown inside bars.\nL30: Literacy — United States: 12, 16, 29, 31, 13; OECD average: 9, 17, 31, 31, 12.\nL31: Numeracy — United States: 15, 19, 28, 26, 12; OECD average: 9, 16, 31, 31, 14.\nL32: Adaptive Problem Solving — United States: 11, 21, 35, 26, 6; OECD average: 8, 22, 38, 27, 5.\nL33: X-axis runs from 0% to 100% with ticks at 20% intervals.\nL34: </visual_element>\nL35: Note: Percentages may not add up to 100 due to rounding.\nL36: Source: Table A.2.2.\nL37: Older adults (aged 55 to 65) in the United States displayed lower proficiency than 25-34 year-olds in literacy, numeracy and adaptive problem solving (Figure 3). In literacy, adults aged 55-65 scored 17 points lower than 25-34 year-olds (OECD average: 30 points lower). Skill gaps between older and younger adults could reflect ageing effects (see below), but also differences in the quality and quantity of education and training across generations.\nL38: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL39: For young adults still enrolled in initial education or who completed it only recently, the results of the Survey of Adult Skills complement those from school-based assessments and provide useful information about the quality of education systems. In the United States, young adults aged 16 to 24 scored, on average, 261 points in literacy (below the OECD average), 248 points in numeracy (below the OECD average), and 250 points in adaptive problem solving (below the OECD average) (Figure 3).\nL40: Figure 3. Average proficiency in literacy, numeracy, and adaptive problem solving, by age\nL41: \nL42: The United States and OECD average\nL43: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL44: Three small line charts titled Literacy, Numeracy, and Adaptive Problem Solving, each comparing United States (blue solid line) with OECD average (gray dashed line) across age groups 16–24, 25–34, 35–44, 45–54, and 55–65. Y-axis is Score points. Shaded bands indicate 95% confidence intervals. Literacy and numeracy peak around ages 25–34 and decline with age; adaptive problem solving also peaks around 25–34 and declines thereafter.\nL45: </visual_element>\nL46: Note: The shaded area represents a measure of uncertainty associated with estimates (the 95% confidence interval).\nL47: Source: Table A.2.4.\nL48: \nL49: How did adults’ skills evolve over the past decade?\nL50: In the United States, average results in 2022-23 went down compared to 2012/15 in literacy and numeracy (Table A.3.1). In both literacy and numeracy, the share of low-performing adults (scoring at Level 1 or below) increased. Meanwhile, the share of high-performing adults (scoring at Levels 4 or 5) remained stable (Figure 4).\nL51: In both domains, therefore, trends were more negative at the lower end of the distribution, and the gap between the highest- and lowest-performing adults widened between 2012/15 and 2022-23 (Tables A.3.3).\nL52: Figure 4. Share of adults scoring at low and high proficiency levels in literacy and numeracy\nL53: \nL54: The United States, 2012/15 and 2022-23\nL55: \nL56: <visual_element id=\"e2\">\nL57: Horizontal bar chart comparing Cycle 1 and Cycle 2 for Literacy and Numeracy. Legend: gray = Level 1 and below; blue = Level 4 and above. Literacy: Cycle 1 low 18, high 13; Cycle 2 low 27, high 13. Numeracy: Cycle 1 low 28, high 10; Cycle 2 low 34, high 12. X-axis is percentage from 0% to 100%.\nL58: </visual_element>\nL59: Note: Does not include adults who in Cycle 2 were only administered the doorstep interview due to a language barrier, to maximise the comparability across cycles.\nL60: Source: Table A.3.2.\nL61: \nL62: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL63: Comparing how adults born in the same years performed across different cycles, and, therefore, at different ages, provides insights into how skills change, on average, as people age. In most countries, these comparisons reveal substantial age-related skill losses after the age of 35 (but more rarely among younger adults). Age-related skill losses are also observed in the United States.\nL64: Young adults born between 1989 and 1996 scored 4 points lower in literacy in 2022-23 (when they were 27-34 years old) than in 2012/15 (when they were 16-23 years old), a non-significant change. Meanwhile, older generations, aged 44 to 54 in 2012/15, scored 19 points lower in literacy in 2022-23 (when they were 55-65 years old), a significant decline (Figure 5).\nL65: Figure 5. Effect of ageing on literacy proficiency in the United States\nL66: \nL67: Change in literacy proficiency within birth cohorts (2012/15 to 2022-23)\nL68: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL69: Horizontal bar chart of score-point differences by birth cohort. Y-axis labels: “Adults born 1989 to 1996 (27-34 year-olds in Cycle 2)”, “Adults born 1979 to 1988 (35-44 year-olds in Cycle 2)”, “Adults born 1969 to 1978 (45-54 year-olds in Cycle 2)”, “Adults born 1958 to 1968 (55-65 year-olds in Cycle 2)”. Bar values shown: -4, -16, -16, -19. X-axis labeled “Score-point difference” with scale from about -50 to 20 and a vertical zero line; the -4 bar is in a lighter tone, the others darker blue.\nL70: </visual_element>\nL71: Note: Does not include adults who in Cycle 2 were only administered the doorstep interview due to a language barrier, to maximise the comparability across cycles; does not include foreign-born adults who had lived in the country less than 10 years. Any non-significant differences are shown in a lighter tone.\nL72: Source: Table A.3.10 (L).\nL73: \nL74: Skill differences related to educational attainment, gender and immigrant background\nL75: In all countries and economies, higher levels of educational attainment are associated with greater proficiency in literacy, numeracy and adaptive problem solving. In the United States, among adults aged 25-65, those with tertiary education scored 46 points higher in literacy than those with upper secondary education (OECD average: 33 points), and those with upper secondary education scored 57 points higher than those with less than upper secondary education (OECD average: 43 points) (Table A.2.5 (L)).\nL76: On average, across participating OECD countries and economies, women displayed higher average proficiency than men in literacy (by 3 points), while men scored higher in numeracy (by 10 points) and adaptive problem solving (by 2 points). In the United States, no significant difference was observed in literacy; a significant 8-point difference in favour of men was observed in numeracy; and no significant difference was observed in adaptive problem solving (Table A.2.7).\nL77: Numeracy proficiency in the United States declined more strongly among men than among women, and the gender gap in numeracy narrowed as a result. Declines were particularly pronounced among older men (compared to women of the same age) (Table A.3.5).\nL78: \nL79: Native-born adults of native-born parents displayed higher proficiency in literacy than foreign-born adults of foreign-born parents (Table A.2.10 (L)). Some of this gap, however, is accounted for by the different\nL80: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL81: socio-demographic characteristics of these two groups. After accounting for other relevant socio-demographic factors, in the United States the gap between the two groups shrinks from 56 to 33 points in literacy (Table A.2.10 (L)). In the United States, native-born adults of native-born parents make up 70% of the population that participated in the Survey of Adult Skills, while the second group (foreign-born adults of foreign-born parents) accounts for 17% of the population. (Table B.3.10).\nL82: How do skills relate to economic and social outcomes in the United States?\nL83: \nL84: Skills have a major impact on lives. In general, higher skills bring significant economic and social benefits. Adults with higher skills tend to have higher educational qualifications; however, the benefits of higher skills extend above and beyond the opportunities associated merely with formal educational qualifications.\nL85: \nL86: Skills are key drivers of employability and wages\nL87: In the United States, as well as on average across OECD countries, adults who score at the highest levels of the numeracy proficiency scale have significantly better employment opportunities compared to adults who score at or below Level 1 (Figure 6).\nL88: \nL89: Figure 6. Employment outcomes in the United States, by numeracy proficiency level\nL90: \nL91: Adults aged 25-65 not in formal education\nL92: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL93: Chart legend: diamond = Level 1 and below; filled circle = Level 4 and above. Three horizontal comparisons for adults aged 25–65 not in formal education:\nL94: - Labour force participation rate (% of working-age population): 62 for Level 1 and below; 93 for Level 4 and above.\nL95: - Unemployment rate (% of active population): 7 for Level 1 and below; 2 for Level 4 and above.\nL96: - Median hourly earnings (Gross, in USD PPP): 19 for Level 1 and below; 42 for Level 4 and above.\nL97: </visual_element>\nL98: Note: Does not include adults who were only administered the doorstep interview due to a language barrier.\nL99: Source: Tables A.4.2 (N) and A.4.7 (N).\nL100: In the United States, differences in employment outcomes by skill level persist even when comparing adults with similar education attainment (and after accounting for other differences that might be associated with higher skills).\nL101: After accounting for these differences, a one-standard-deviation increase in numeracy proficiency is associated with a 7-percentage point higher likelihood of participating in the labour force (Table A.4.5) And, among employed adults, a one-standard-deviation increase in numeracy proficiency is associated with wages that are 11% higher, a statistically significant difference.\nL102: For comparison, a one-standard-deviation increase in education is associated with wages that are 21% higher in the United States (Table A.4.8).\nL103: How individual well-being and civic engagement relate to skills\nL104: \nL105: Skills are closely related to both individual well-being (e.g. self-reported health and life satisfaction) and civic engagement (e.g. political efficacy, trust and volunteering). Many low-skilled adults feel disconnected\nL106: \nL107: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL108: \nL109: 6\nL110: \nL111: from political processes and lack the skills to engage with complex digital information, which is a growing concern for modern democracies.\nL112: Adults who score at the highest levels of the proficiency scale were significantly more likely to report high levels of life satisfaction and being in very good or excellent health than adults who score at or below Level 1, in the United States as well as on average across OECD countries (Figure 7 and Table A.4.10 (N)).\nL113: In the United States, this positive relationship between life satisfaction, health and numeracy holds even after controlling for a number of personal characteristics (age, gender, years of education, immigrant background, parental educational attainment, and whether an individual lives with a partner or has children) (Table A.4.11 (N)).\nL114: \nL115: **Figure 7. Well-being and civic outcomes in the United States, by numeracy proficiency level**\nL116: \nL117: Adults aged 25-65 not in formal education\nL138: *A good match between the skills and qualifications of workers and those required by their jobs is essential for a well-functioning and productive economy*\nL139: \nL140: In OECD countries, many workers are mismatched to their jobs, meaning that their qualifications, skills, or fields of study are different from what their current job would require. Mismatches can result from an\nL141: \nL142: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL143: \nL144: |7\nL145: inefficient allocation of workers to jobs. They can also reflect the fact that workforce skills and qualifications are not keeping pace with structural changes in the economy, driven by digitalisation, an ageing population, and the green transition.\nL146: \nL147: Figure 8. Mismatches in qualifications, skills and field of study\nL148: \nL149: Employed adults aged 25-65 who are not self-employed\nL152: Three mismatch categories are shown with left-side labels and right-side percentages: Qualification mismatch (Under-qualified: United States 9, OECD average 9; Over-qualified: United States 26, OECD average 23), Skill mismatch (Under-skilled: United States 8, OECD average 10; Over-skilled: United States 39, OECD average 26), and Field-of-study mismatch (Mismatched: United States 38, OECD average 38). X-axis runs from 0% to 100%.\nL154: Note: Does not include adults who were only administered the doorstep interview due to a language barrier.\nL155: Source: Table A.4.12.\nL156: \nL157: In the United States, about 26% of workers are over-qualified (OECD average: 23%), and a further 9% are under-qualified (OECD average: 9%), meaning their highest educational qualification is above or below the level that is typically required for their current job (Figure 8).\nL158: About 8% of workers report that some of their skills are lower than what is required for their job (OECD average: 10%) (Figure 8). In the United States, they often say this is because they need to improve their computer or software skills (43%), followed by skills in handling customers/clients, patients or students (31%) (Table A.4.13).\nL159: \nL160: Finally, 38% of workers are mismatched in terms of field of study, because their highest qualification is not in the field that is most relevant to their job (Figure 8).\nL161: In OECD countries, adults who are overqualified for their job incur significant economic and social costs. This is also the case in the United States: on average, their wages are 19% lower than peers in well-matched jobs who have similar educational attainment (OECD average: 12% lower) (Table A.4.16) and they are 12 percentage points less likely to report a high level of life satisfaction (OECD average: 4 percentage points less likely) (Table A.4.17).\nL165: The Survey of Adult Skills collects data through a personal interview and a self-completed assessment. As a household survey, data collection takes place in the respondents’ own homes. In the United States, 3765 adults participated in the survey (this reflects an overall response rate of 28%). The sample was drawn to be representative of about 213.3 million 16-to-65-year-olds residing in the country at the time of data collection, irrespective of nationality, citizenship or language status.\nL167: SURVEY OF ADULT SKILLS 2023 – COUNTRY NOTES: UNITED STATES © OECD 2024\nL168: \nL169: The assessment\nL170: \nL171: The 2023 Survey of Adult Skills assessed adults in three domains: literacy, numeracy, and adaptive problem solving. The assessments required adults to complete a set of tasks that reflect how these skills are applied across a wide range of situations in adults’ lives. Many tasks involve data-intensive, complex digital environments, which are increasingly common in the workplace and everyday life in modern societies.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJapan’s Legislation for Peace and Security (https://www.mofa.go.jp/files/000143304.pdf)\nciteturn71view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10.6 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn56search13\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 196\nL0: Japan’s Legislation for Peace and Security\nL1: \nL2: Seamless Responses for Peace and Security of Japan and the International Community\nL3: \nL4: March 2016\nL5: \nL6: Government of Japan\nL7: \nL8: 1. Backgrounds\nL9: - Fundamental transformation of the security environment surrounding Japan\nL10:   (e.g. Shift in the global power balance, rapid progress of technological innovation, development and proliferation of weapons of mass destruction/ballistic missiles, international terrorism, etc.)\nL11: - No country can secure its own peace only by itself.\nL12: - The international community expects Japan to play a more proactive role for peace and stability in the world, in a way commensurate with its national capability.\nL13: - → Need to develop domestic legislation that enables seamless responses\nL14: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL15: A left-to-right blue arrow timeline with five rounded blue boxes showing the sequence of Japan’s security legislation process:\nL16: 1. “Cabinet Decision on Development of Seamless Security Legislation to Ensure Japan’s Survival and Protect its People” (July 2014)\nL17: 2. Discussion in the Government and the ruling coalition debate in the Diet\nL18: 3. Submission of draft legislation to the Diet and its deliberations (May 2015~)\nL19: 4. Approval of the legislation by the Diet (September 19, 2015)\nL20: 5. The legislation took effect (March 29, 2016)\nL21: </visual_element>\nL22: - The legislation will enable seamless responses to any situations to secure the lives and peaceful livelihood of the Japanese people. Japan will be able to contribute more proactively to the peace and stability of the international community under the policy of “Proactive Contribution to Peace” based on the principle of international cooperation.\nL23: - There is no change in Japan’s basic posture and orientation for the past 70 years, including a peace-loving nation.\nL24: - The legislation will enhance the deterrence of the Japan-U.S. Alliance for the peace and stability in the Asia-Pacific region. It will also help to deepen trust and cooperative relations with other partners both within/outside the region.\nL25: - Japan will continue to make its best efforts to promote deeper understanding of countries concerned and ensure transparency.\nL26: 2. Further Contribution to the Peace and Stability of the International Community ①\nL27: \nL28: A. Support Activities\nL29: \nL30: The SDF will be able to provide necessary logistics support and search & rescue to armed forces of foreign countries collectively addressing the situation which threatens the international peace and security, under a certain U.N. resolution*.\nL31: \nL32: <Precedents> Special measures law for anti-terrorism and Iraq (expired)\nL33: →<New> Permanent law\nL34: *(1) A resolution which decides, calls upon, recommends, or authorizes actions against threats to the peace and security of the international community.\nL35: (2) In addition to (1), a resolution which recognizes that the situation amounts to the threats to the peace or breaches of the peace, and requests member states to take certain measures in relation to the situation.\nL36: The SDF will be able to provide necessary logistics support and search & rescue to armed forces of foreign countries engaging in activities for ensuring Japan’s peace and security in situations that will have an important influence on Japan’s peace and security.\nL37: \nL38: <Current> Limited to the U.S. Armed Forces\nL39: →<New> Armed Forces of Foreign Countries (including the U.S.)\nL40: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL41: Left side: orange soldiers under a gray diagonal area and green soldiers above it, with the label “Units of other countries armed forces”; a red arrow points from the right toward the green soldiers.\nL42: Center text lists logistics support items: “Supply (expansion of items, including ammunition, oil, fuel)”, “Transportation”, “Medical Services etc.” Right side: a blue oval encloses icons of a building, truck, barrels, medical box, Japanese flag, ships/aircraft, and a road/bridge, with a blue upward arrow and a red arrow indicating movement/support flow.\nL43: </visual_element>\nL44: *Exception: Logistics support in “the scene where combat activities are conducted at the time”\nL45: \nL46: *Restriction of the area: choosing an operation area where the SDF can smoothly and safely provide logistics support\nL47: \nL48: <New> Provision of ammunition, maintenance and fueling for aircraft which is ready to take off for combat operation\nL49: \nL50: 2\nL51: \nL52: 2. Further Contribution to the Peace and Stability of the International Community ②\nL53: \nL54: B.(1) International Peace Cooperation Activities\nL55: Additional roles by Japanese Corps in operation to respond to changes in role and responsibility of UN PKOs, including the protection of civilians.\nL56: \nL57: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL58: Photo of UN peacekeepers and civilians wading through floodwater; several helmeted personnel escort people, including a child being carried, in a flooded outdoor area.\nL59: </visual_element>\nL60: <visual_element id=\"e2\">\nL61: Light-blue rounded text panel with two dashed-outline note boxes and underlined headings describing changes in UN PKO roles and rules of engagement.\nL62: </visual_element>\nL63: \nL64: <visual_element id=\"e3\">\nL65: Photo of UN personnel and local civilians beside a white vehicle marked “U N” in a dry settlement area.\nL66: </visual_element>\nL67: <visual_element id=\"e4\">\nL68: Top note box text:\nL69: <Current> Tasks to assist observation of cease-fire agreements, assistance in elections, assistance to people suffering due to conflict, etc.\nL70: →<New> Addition of tasks in protection of local populations, protection of individuals related to operations and other advisory activities, etc.\nL71: </visual_element>\nL72: <visual_element id=\"e5\">\nL73: Bottom note box text:\nL74: Revision of ROE for use of weapons to better align with current UN standard.\nL75: <Current> Limited to self-defense and protection of its own weapons/other equipment\nL76: →<New> Revised ROE in defense of the mandate in specific circumstances\nL77: </visual_element>\nL78: \nL79: 2. Further Contribution to the Peace and Stability of the International Community ③\nL80: \nL81: B.(2) International Peace Cooperation Activities\nL82: In addition, Japan will be able to participate in internationally coordinated operations for peace and security (outside U.N. PKO framework) under the following conditions:\nL83: (1) A resolution of the U.N. General Assembly, Security Council, or Economic and Social Council\nL84: or\nL85: (2) A request by any of the following international organizations:\nL86: &nbsp;&nbsp;a. The United Nations\nL87: &nbsp;&nbsp;b. Organizations established by the U.N. General Assembly, or U.N. Specialized Agencies, such as the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR) and otherwise specified by a Cabinet Order\nL88: &nbsp;&nbsp;c. Regional organizations prescribed in the Article 52 of the UN Charter or organizations established by multilateral treaties, having the actual achievements or expertise pertaining to the activities of internationally coordinated operation for peace and security such as the European Union and otherwise specified by a Cabinet Order\nL89: or\nL90: (3) Requests of countries to which the area where those operations are to be conducted belongs (limited to only those cases that are supported by any of the principal organs of the U.N. as prescribed in the Article 7(1) of the U.N. Charter)\nL91: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL92: Right-side dashed rounded box showing the UN Peacekeeping logo and text “United Nations Peacekeeping” with “un.org/peacekeeping”; blue bullet notes “*International humanitarian operations (UNHCR)” and “*International election observation operations (UNDP, OSCE)”; a large plus sign; italic text “In addition… Operations at the requests by”; and three logos at the bottom for the UN Security Council, the European Union, and UNHCR.\nL93: </visual_element>\nL94: <visual_element id=\"e2\">\nL95: Dashed note box stating: “<Current> U.N. PKO, International humanitarian operations, International election observation operations → <New> Wider participation in internationally coordinated operations for peace and security”\nL96: </visual_element>\nL97: \nL98: 2. Further Contribution to the Peace and Stability of the International Community ④\nL99: \nL100: C. Rescue of Japanese Nationals Abroad etc.\nL101: The SDF will be allowed to use weapons to rescue Japanese nationals overseas given the consent of the territorial State and certain other conditions met. Under certain conditions, the SDF will also be able to rescue non-Japanese who is with Japanese nationals to be rescued by SDF.\nL102: \nL103: <Current> Transportation only\nL104: → <New> Guard, rescue etc. in addition to transportation\nL105: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL106: Diagram for “Rescue of Japanese Nationals Abroad etc.” showing:\nL107: - (Country A) “Consent of the territorial state”\nL108: - “Japanese and non-Japanese nationals”\nL109: - “Armed guerilla, terrorist, etc.”\nL110: - “JSDF”\nL111: - A red explosion between armed figures and civilians\nL112: - Red arrows from armed guerilla/terrorist figures toward the explosion\nL113: - Blue dashed arrow from JSDF toward the scene\nL114: - A military vehicle image and an airplane silhouette\nL115: - Japanese flag icon near JSDF\nL116: </visual_element>\nL117: D. Ship Inspection Operations\nL118: \nL119: The SDF will be able to conduct ship inspection operations for the purpose of ensuring peace and stability of the international community.\nL120: \nL121: <Current> Limited to situations that will have an important influence on Japan’s peace and security\nL122: → <New> For international peace and security purpose\nL123: <visual_element id=\"e2\">\nL124: Diagram for “Ship Inspection Operations” showing:\nL125: - Ocean photo\nL126: - Text box: “Ship inspection under the consent of the ship’s captain”\nL127: - UN emblem with a downward arrow\nL128: - Text: “Request for ship inspection or consent of the flag state of the ship”\nL129: - Ship illustration and cargo ship illustration with a left-pointing arrow between them\nL130: - Text: “Possibility of carrying weapons to conflict area...”\nL131: - Japanese flag icon near the ship\nL132: </visual_element>\nL133: 3. Measures for Self-Defense Permitted Under the Constitution\nL134: \nL135: The GOJ had previously considered that the Constitution permits the “use of force” only when Japan had an “armed attack”.\nL136: \nL137: Given the fundamental transformation of the security environment, in certain situation, an armed attack against a foreign country could threaten Japan’s survival, depending on its purpose, scale and manner.\nL138: The GOJ has reached a conclusion that the “use of force” will be permitted when the following “Three New Conditions” are met:\nL139: \nL140: “Three New Conditions”\nL141: \nL142: (1) When an armed attack against Japan occurs or when an armed attack against a foreign country that is in a close relationship with Japan occurs and as a result threatens Japan’s survival and poses a clear danger to fundamentally overturn people’s right to life, liberty and pursuit of happiness*,\nL143: (2) When there is no other appropriate means available to repel the attack and ensure Japan’s survival and protects its people,\nL144: \nL145: (3) Use of force should be limited to the minimum extent necessary\nL146: \nL147: * As a matter of course, use of force must be carried out while observing international law. In certain situations this is based on the right of collective self-defense under international law.\nL148: \nL149: (Appendix) Matrix of the Legislation for Peace and Security\nL150: Red = New legislation\nL151: Blue = Expansion of current legislation\nL152: \nL153: Peacetime → Contingency\nL154: \nL155: Left vertical axis:\nL156: - Concerning Japan and Japanese Nationals\nL157: - Concerning International Cooperation\nL158: \nL159: Bottom banner:\nL160: - Reorganizing the Agenda Items of the National Security Council (NSC)\nL161: \nL162: Footnote:\nL163: - * Cabinet Decisions on accelerating procedures in cases of responding to a situation where an infringement that does not amount to an armed attack occurs in areas surrounding remote islands etc.\nL164: <visual_element id=\"e1\">\nL165: Matrix diagram with color-coded rounded boxes showing legislation categories across peacetime to contingency and from Japan/nationals to international cooperation.\nL166: Green boxes indicate existing/new rescue and protection measures for Japanese nationals and SDF equipment; blue boxes indicate peacetime support and UN PKO-related expansions; yellow boxes indicate support activities and ship inspection operations; orange box indicates response to armed attack against Japan or a foreign country threatening Japan’s survival; brown dashed boxes indicate new multinational cooperation and collective international peace/security support.\nL167: </visual_element>\nL168: Key boxes and text:\nL169: \nL170: - Transportation of Japanese nationals overseas etc. (Existing)\nL171:   Rescue of Japanese nationals overseas etc. (New)\nL172: \nL173: - Protection of SDF’s weapons/other equipment (Existing)\nL174:   Protection of weapons/other equipment of U.S. and other countries’ armed forces (New)\nL175: \nL176: - Provision of supplies/services to the U.S. Armed Forces in peacetime (Expansion)\nL177:   - Expand situations where supplies/services can be provided\nL178: - International Peace Cooperation Activities\nL179:   UN PKO (Expansion)\nL180:   - Additional roles by Japanese Corps in operation (e.g. protection of local populations, etc., and protection of individuals related to operations)\nL181:   - Revision of ROE for use of weapons (small arms) to better align with current U.N. standard\nL182: - Support activities (including logistics support) in situations that will have an important influence on Japan’s peace and security (Expansion)\nL183:   - Support activities to armed forces of foreign countries (not only the U.S.)\nL184:   - Expand the scope of support activities\nL185: \nL186: - Ship Inspection Operations (Expansion)\nL187:   - Enable operations for international peace and security\nL188: - Response to an armed attack against Japan (Existing)\nL189:   Response to an armed attack against a foreign country resulting in threatening Japan’s survival (New)\nL190:   → “Use of Force” permitted under the “Three New Conditions”\nL191: \nL192: - Internationally coordinated operations for peace and security (Multinational cooperation outside UN PKO framework) (New)\nL193: - Support activities (including logistics support) to armed forces of foreign countries in situations where the international community is collectively addressing for international peace and security (New)\nL194: \nL195: <Page number: 7>\n"
